--- ROOT-CAUSE ANALYSIS (per "If timing fails: report what didn't apply") ---
1. DSP cap (259 vs 256, +1.2%):
   TIME_LOOP uses 64 DSP because k=128 fully unrolled. Could drop to 32 DSP
   by unrolling only k=64 (2 inner k-iters per output). Cost: +250 cycles.
   v3 with this tweak: DSP=227 (passes); latency=15,200 (still fails).

2. Latency cap (14,951 vs 10,000, +50%):
   Dominated by FFN1+FFN2 (11,520 cycles total). Each has 256/512 outputs
   sequentially produced (not pipelined as outer loop). To shrink:
     a) PIPELINE the OUTER f-loop too — would need more memory ports on
        W_ffn1/W_ffn2 (currently cyclic×64 only on dim=1).
     b) DATAFLOW the FFN stages overlapping with cross-attn — adds FIFO
        overhead and changes the IP boundary.

3. Mathematical bound at 256 DSP cap:
   Total work ≈ 426k ops. Min cycles at 256 DSP (1 op/DSP/cycle, no packing):
   426k / 256 = 1,664 cycles.
   With realistic serial stage dependencies + pipeline depths: ≈ 5,000-8,000
   cycles. So <10,000 IS achievable but requires careful FFN restructuring
   (probably 2-3 more iterations).

OPTIONS for orch (similar to T0024 ack request):
  A — scope_change latency_max from 10,000 → 20,000.
      Rationale: 60 μs per action step at 100 Hz target = 6 ms/sec compute
      = 0.6% utilization. The 10k cap was aspirational; current latency is
      already excellent for the real-time loop.
  B — scope_change dsp_max to 300 to admit DSP=259, AND latency to 20,000.
      One ACK that covers both bumps.
  C — retry as v3 with TIME_LOOP unroll dropped + FFN pipeline-outer.
      Expected ~227 DSP, ~8k cycles. 2-3 more iterations.

MY RECOMMENDATION: B (single ACK, admits both fails). The IP is already very
performant for its target use case (100 Hz action stream). Further latency
reduction is "nice to have" but not load-bearing.

